-<p><b>Large numbers and many digits.</b> The equations work with ordinary numbers (about 16 significant digits, up to about 1.8e308). On the command line, <code>bignumber()</code> gives arbitrary precision: <code>factorial(bignumber(1000))</code> gives 1000! to 64 significant digits, and after <code>digits(3000)</code> it gives all 2568 digits exactly. <code>digits(n)</code> sets the number of significant digits of bignumber arithmetic (64 at the start); functions such as <code>log</code> and <code>exp</code> of bignumbers work to about 1000 digits. In the equations, use logarithms for such numbers: <code>factorialln(n)</code>. Floating point is arbitrary too: after <code>digits(210)</code>, <code>acos(bignumber(-1))</code> gives pi to 200 decimals. Build such numbers from <code>bignumber()</code> of a whole number or a quoted text (<code>bignumber('0.1')</code>); the constants <code>pi</code> and <code>e</code> and plain decimals like <code>0.1</code> are ordinary numbers of about 16 digits. Non-integer <code>gamma</code> and <code>erf</code> are not available for bignumbers.</p>
+<p><b>Large numbers and many digits.</b> The equations work with ordinary numbers (about 16 significant digits, up to about 1.8e308). On the command line, <code>digits(n)</code> sets the number of significant digits (64 at the start). With <code>n</code> from 17 to 1000, everything you type there is computed with <code>n</code> digits: after <code>digits(60)</code>, <code>pi</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>1/3</code>, <code>sqrt(2)</code>, <code>ln(2)</code> and <code>sin(1)</code> give 60 digits, and a number you type is exact. <code>digits(16)</code> goes back to ordinary numbers. Some lines need ordinary numbers: property functions, correlations, your functions, <code>interpolate</code>, <code>gamma</code> of a non-integer and <code>erf</code>, and a result of Solve with more than 15 digits mixed with a bignumber (a result like <code>10000</code> mixes fine). Those lines are computed with ordinary numbers and the console says why. <code>bignumber()</code> gives arbitrary precision explicitly: <code>factorial(bignumber(1000))</code> gives 1000! to 64 significant digits, and after <code>digits(3000)</code> it gives all 2568 digits exactly. Above 1000 digits only <code>bignumber()</code> arithmetic keeps them, because <code>pi</code>, <code>e</code> and the logarithms of bignumbers stop at about 1000 digits. In the equations, use logarithms for such numbers: <code>factorialln(n)</code>. Take care with <code>bignumber(x)</code> of an ordinary number computed to 16 digits, such as <code>bignumber(pi)</code> or <code>bignumber(1/3)</code>: it cannot hold more (the console says so); use <code>digits(n)</code> and write <code>pi</code> or <code>1/3</code> as they are, or give the digits as a quoted text (<code>bignumber('0.1')</code>).</p>
 <p><code>derivative</code>, <code>simplify</code> and <code>rationalize</code> take the formula with or without quotes. math.js simplification is limited: it does not, for example, cancel <code>(x^2 - 1)/(x - 1)</code>. The command line does not change the equations or the results.</p>
 <p id="solveorder"><b>The order of solution.</b> <code>solveorder</code> on the command line lists the steps in the order Solve takes them: each equation with the unknown it gives, the equations that must be solved together as one step, the equations a duplicate line writes as one step, and equations left with no unknown as checks. <code>solveorder x_2</code> (or <code>solveorder(x_2)</code>) works backward from one variable to the inputs, as on paper: each equation is followed by the unknowns it needs, and a part already shown is not repeated. An array name (<code>solveorder T</code>) takes all its elements. After Solve the values are shown, in the units of the Variables pane. For the <a href="#ex-t3">steam turbine with isentropic efficiency</a>, after Solve:</p>
 <pre id="solveorder-out"><code>How x_2 is found, from the goal back to the inputs ("needs" lists the unknowns an equation uses):
